# Architecture Principles

These principles apply throughout CampusFlow.

## 1. Clarity over cleverness

Prefer readable, obvious code over sophisticated abstractions.

## 2. SOLID

Apply SOLID where it improves maintainability, not mechanically.

## 3. Feature-first organization

Organize business functionality by feature first and responsibility
second.

Example:

```text
features/
└── students/
    ├── api/
    ├── components/
    ├── hooks/
    ├── mocks/
    ├── pages/
    ├── types/
    └── validation/
```

## 4. Just-in-time infrastructure

Do not create folders, interfaces, services, or frameworks without a
real requirement.

## 5. The second implementation earns the abstraction

A repeated pattern should be observed in real code before becoming
shared infrastructure.

## 6. API contract first

APIs should use explicit contracts and versioned routes such as:

```text
/api/v1/...
```

## 7. Documentation is part of Definition of Done

Every meaningful story should include:

- Source code
- Pull request
- Tests where applicable
- Documentation update where applicable
- Architecture Decision Record when an architectural decision is
  introduced

## 8. Git workflow

Base branch:

```text
develop
```

Typical workflow:

```text
develop
   ↓
feature/ST-xxx-description
   ↓
Implementation
   ↓
Build / Test
   ↓
Commit
   ↓
Push
   ↓
Pull Request → develop
   ↓
Review
   ↓
Merge
```

